nd to understand  the dynamic between peter and doctor  strange and so on but taken as a whole  it's a lot to ask of the average viewer  at this point how many people who saw  the amazing spider-man 2 will remember  the finer points of the history between  electro and andrew garfield's spider-man  and after 14 years we might need a  little more than a line or two's worth  of refresher on what sandman's whole  deal with his daughter was in spider-man  3. obviously the mcu was built on a new  strategy of connecting the movies in a  larger universe and this worked a great  effect but each installment still has to  serve as its own story no way home  depends almost entirely on your  knowledge of other movies to a dizzying  extent kids who have been keeping up  will have a blast but any adult who's  been on enough spider-man will have a  seriously hard time keeping up with the  subtext of what's even happening it's  one thing for movies to bank on your  nostalgia but it's another thing to make  a viewer feel left out like they haven't  done their homework if you're a viewer  who's getting older and maybe started to  check out of comic book movies  spider-man no way home is like expert  level nostalgia designed to challenge  your memory of about a dozen movies from  decades past so you know too  it's cool um i mean i've known first and  i'm no longer but it's not a competition  if there's one crime the mcu has  committed it's the popularizing of mid  and post credit scenes after four phases  and two dozen movies marvel studios has  us trained on mass to wait through all  the credits for one or two more glimpses  of what the future instalments and the  series have to offer spider-man no way  home has a mid-credits scene with a fun  cameo that's worth the wait but that  makes you wait all the way until the end  for a teaser trailer for doctor strange  and the multiverse of madness it's not  even a post-credits scene called footage  or not it's just an advertisement even  by